<h2>The C++ Type Loophole (C++14)</h2>

<p>Recently I was doing some work on retrieving struct data member types. A=
nd as I was exploring,=20
  researching and testing ideas I uncovered this pearl:</p>

<pre><code class=3D"hljs cpp"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">template</span>&=
lt;<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">int</span> N&gt; <span class=3D"hljs-keywor=
d">struct</span> tag{};

<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">template</span>&lt;<span class=3D"hljs-keyword=
">typename</span> T, <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">int</span> N&gt;
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">struct</span> <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">loo=
phole_t</span> {
  <span class=3D"hljs-function"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">friend</span> =
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">auto</span> <span class=3D"hljs-title">loophol=
e</span><span class=3D"hljs-params">(tag&lt;N&gt;)</span> </span>{ return T=
{}; };
};

<span class=3D"hljs-function"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">auto</span> <spa=
n class=3D"hljs-title">loophole</span><span class=3D"hljs-params">(tag&lt;<=
span class=3D"hljs-number">0</span>&gt;)</span></span>;

<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">sizeof</span>( <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">lo=
ophole_t</span>&lt;<span class=3D"hljs-built_in">std</span>::<span class=3D=
"hljs-built_in">string</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">0</span>&gt; );

=3D=3D=3D&gt; <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">std</span>::is_same&lt; <span c=
lass=3D"hljs-built_in">std</span>::<span class=3D"hljs-built_in">string</sp=
an>, <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">decltype</span>( loophole(tag&lt;<span cl=
ass=3D"hljs-number">0</span>&gt;{}) ) &gt;::value; &lt;=3D=3D=3D
</code></pre>

<p><strong>What is the result you think? TRUE!</strong> Check it <a href=3D=
"https://godbolt.org/g/PHjMPC" title=3D"The Great Type Loophole">here at go=
dbolt.org</a>.</p>

<p>Interesting. I had so much pain creating <a href=3D"https://alexpolt.git=
hub.io/struct-tuple.html" title=3D"Structure Data Members as a Type List Us=
ing Pure C++">struct reader</a> to detect data member types of
  a struct. And it was painfully limited: only literal types, pre-built typ=
e list, etc. With this=20
  anything becomes possible.</p>

<p>After some compiler-fight I came up with an elegant ten lines of code he=
ader that can create a
  type list from a data structure (with certain limitations) with just C++,=
 no fixed type lists.
  Here is a short example of what it can do:</p>

<pre><code class=3D"hljs glsl">using wndclass_tlist =3D loophole_ns::as_typ=
e_list&lt; WNDCLASS &gt;;

Resulting type list (Visual Studio <span class=3D"hljs-number">2017</span>)=
:

  <span class=3D"hljs-number">0.</span> unsigned <span class=3D"hljs-type">=
int</span>
  <span class=3D"hljs-number">1.</span> __int64 (__cdecl*)(struct HWND__ * =
__ptr64,unsigned <span class=3D"hljs-type">int</span>,unsigned __int64,__in=
t64)
  <span class=3D"hljs-number">2.</span> <span class=3D"hljs-type">int</span=
>
  <span class=3D"hljs-number">3.</span> <span class=3D"hljs-type">int</span=
>
  <span class=3D"hljs-number">4.</span> struct HINSTANCE__ * __ptr64
  <span class=3D"hljs-number">5.</span> struct HICON__ * __ptr64
  <span class=3D"hljs-number">6.</span> struct HICON__ * __ptr64
  <span class=3D"hljs-number">7.</span> struct HBRUSH__ * __ptr64
  <span class=3D"hljs-number">8.</span> wchar_t <span class=3D"hljs-keyword=
">const</span> * __ptr64
  <span class=3D"hljs-number">9.</span> wchar_t <span class=3D"hljs-keyword=
">const</span> * __ptr64
</code></pre>

<p></p><h3>Isn't it awesome!?</h3> <p></p>

<p>This type loophole basically allows us to store some type into a "variab=
le" and later read=20
  it back! It's very powerful.</p>

<p>More examples: we can turn a structure into luple: a lightweight tuple o=
f my design that has=20
  the advantage of a stable layout across compilers (at least as far as I t=
ested it):</p>

<pre><code class=3D"hljs cpp"><span class=3D"hljs-keyword">struct</span> da=
ta {
  <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">float</span> a;
  <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">std</span>::<span class=3D"hljs-built_in">s=
tring</span> b;
};

<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">using</span> data_tlist =3D loophole_ns::as_ty=
pe_list&lt; data &gt;;
<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">using</span> data_luple =3D luple&lt; data_tli=
st &gt;;

data d{ <span class=3D"hljs-number">1.f</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-string"=
>"Hello World!"</span> };

<span class=3D"hljs-keyword">auto</span>&amp; l =3D <span class=3D"hljs-key=
word">reinterpret_cast</span>&lt; wndclass_luple&amp; &gt;( d ); <span clas=
s=3D"hljs-comment">//I know what you feel)</span>

get&lt; <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">std</span>::<span class=3D"hljs-built=
_in">string</span> &gt;(l) =3D <span class=3D"hljs-string">"Welcome, New Wo=
rld!"</span>

luple_do( l, []( auto&amp; value ) { std::cout &lt;&lt; value &lt;&lt; <spa=
n class=3D"hljs-string">"\n"</span>; } );
</code></pre>

<p>If you want to know more about how it works and its limitations then che=
ck out the code.=20
  I provide full <strong>commented source code</strong> of both the type lo=
ophole and luple in the=20
  <a href=3D"https://github.com/alexpolt/luple" title=3D"Repository for Typ=
e Loophole and Luple">GitHub repo</a>. It works on all three major compiler=
s.</p>

<p>You can see it in action online at <a href=3D"https://goo.gl/2T5Ww6" tit=
le=3D"Demonstration of the Great Type Loophole at Work at tio.run">tio.run<=
/a>, <a href=3D"http://coliru.stacked-crooked.com/a/69ee9bdf2bb4dc75" title=
=3D"Demonstration of the Great Type Loophole at Work at Coliru">Coliru</a>,=
 <a href=3D"https://wandbox.org/permlink/kKUjvDQMmSR4gnz7" title=3D"Demonst=
ration of the Great Type Loophole at Work at Wandbox">Wandbox</a>.</p>

<p>You can find a more condensed example of a data member type detector usi=
ng type loophole=20
  <a href=3D"https://godbolt.org/g/bxcKZY" title=3D"Member Type Detector us=
ing Type Loophole">here at godbolt.org</a> (a slightly other version was in=
itially <a href=3D"https://www.reddit.com/r/cpp/comments/6ydxya/the_great_t=
ype_loophole/dmn50vn/" title=3D"Type Loophole at Reddit">provided</a> by a=
=20
  user named jguegant at Reddit).</p>

<p><a href=3D"https://github.com/sasha-s" title=3D"Sasha Sobol GitHub">Sash=
a Sobol</a> has come up with an example (<a href=3D"http://coliru.stacked-c=
rooked.com/a/6094c5aa5e75e240" title=3D"Compile Time Type IDs using Type Lo=
ophole">online at Coliru</a>) that makes it possible=20
  to assign unique IDs to types during compile time (it's instantiation ord=
er dependent).</p>

<p>If you're not comfortable with reading the source code then some nice gu=
y (render787) on Reddit=20
  made a <a href=3D"https://www.reddit.com/r/cpp/comments/6ydxya/the_great_=
type_loophole/dmmoveu/">detailed comment</a> that gives a good observation =
of how this loophole allows the=20
  struct data member type information to be acquired. </p>

<p>I have not so far examined if that's in violation of the Standard. So, C=
++ Standard gurus are
  really welcome. The first to <a href=3D"https://twitter.com/TartanLlama/s=
tatus/905306427929001986" title=3D"Type Loophole Twitter Post">respond at T=
witter</a> was <a href=3D"https://twitter.com/TartanLlama" title=3D"Simon B=
rand">Simon Brand</a> who pointed to a=20
  defect report <a href=3D"http://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/cwg_=
active.html#2118" title=3D"Core Working Group Defect Report 2118">2118</a>.=
</p>

<p>Follow me on <a href=3D"https://twitter.com/poltavsky_alex" title=3D"Ale=
xandr Poltavsky, Software Developer">Twitter</a> for updates about my new a=
rticles (mostly programming and computer
  graphics). </p>

